    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.modular_decomposition - EdgeCase\",\n    \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.modular_decomposition.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.modular_decomposition()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Build the modular decomposition tree of an undirected graph.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.modular_decomposition()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Build the modular decomposition tree of an undirected graph.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.modular_decomposition()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Build the modular decomposition tree of an undirected graph.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nBuild the modular decomposition tree of an undirected graph.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L33992-L34152){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.modular_decomposition()\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Modular decomposition](../assets/graphs/modular-decomposition.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Left: a graph where the clique A, B, C, D is joined to E, E is joined to F, G and H, and those three are joined to I. Its two strong modules with several nodes are tinted: A, B, C, D in the accent color and F, G, H in teal. Right: the decomposition tree. The prime root M1 has four children: the series module M2 (accent) over A, B, C, D, the leaf E, the parallel module M3 (teal) over F, G, H, and the leaf I.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nA *module* is a set of nodes that every outside node treats alike: it is adjacent to\nall of them or to none. Modules are the parts of a graph that can be collapsed to a\nsingle node without losing information, much as a group of interchangeable members\nacts as one unit to the rest of a network. The *strong* modules, those that overlap\nno other module, nest into a single tree, the modular decomposition, which describes\nevery module of the graph.\n\nEach tree node is a strong module. Its leaves are the single nodes of the graph, and\neach internal node has one of three types, chosen by how its children connect:\n\n- `\"parallel\"`: the module is disconnected, and its children are its components.\n- `\"series\"`: the complement of the module is disconnected, so every child is joined\n  to every other, and its children are the complement's components.\n- `\"prime\"`: neither, and its children are its maximal proper modules. Collapsing them\n  leaves a graph with no modules other than single nodes and the whole graph.\n\nThe result is a new directed graph with an edge from each module to each of its\nchildren. Leaves keep the labels of the nodes they hold and internal nodes are\nlabeled `\"M1\"`, `\"M2\"`, ... in preorder, so `\"M1\"` is the root unless the graph has a\nsingle node (then the tree is that one leaf). If some node of the graph is already\nlabeled like that, the prefix is doubled (`\"MM1\"`, ...) until the labels are distinct.\nEvery tree node has a `type` attribute (`\"parallel\"`, `\"series\"`, `\"prime\"` or\n`\"leaf\"`) and a `nodes` attribute listing the labels of the original nodes in the\nmodule. Children appear in the order of their smallest node ID.\n\nSelf-loops and parallel edges are ignored.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   A new directed, unweighted graph holding the decomposition tree. The empty graph\n    gives an empty tree.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If the graph is directed.\n:::\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~is_cograph`) \n\n:   Whether the tree has no prime nodes.\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nNetworkX pull request #7957 (not yet merged) ports an existing C++ implementation;\nthis one is written independently from the definitions. A module that is\ndisconnected, or whose complement is, splits into the components (found in\n$O(V + E)$ time, or by a complement search over the unvisited nodes). Otherwise the\nmodule is prime, and its children come from the vertex partitioning of Ehrenfeucht et\nal. (1994): partition refinement from a pivot node $v$ splits the other nodes into\nthe maximal modules that avoid $v$, in time linear in their edges, and a forcing\ndigraph on those parts (part $X$ forces $Y$ when $Y$ tells $X$ apart from $v$) has a\nunique source component made of the parts outside the largest proper module holding\n$v$. The children are those parts plus that module. The tree is unique, so the result\nis the same as NetworkX's (which reports the root separately and accepts a pivot\npicker that cannot change the answer). Each level costs near-linear time for sparse\nmodules; deep trees on dense graphs, such as threshold graphs, approach $O(V^3)$.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\n`B` and `C` are both linked to `A` and to nothing else, so every other node treats them\nalike: they form a module. The root is prime, since neither the graph nor its complement\nfalls apart into pieces:\n\n::: {#03943fc6 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"A\", \"C\"), (\"A\", \"D\"), (\"D\", \"E\")]\n)\ntree = graph.modular_decomposition()\n\ntree.get_node_df().select(\"label\", \"type\", \"nodes\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```{=html}\n<div><style>\n.dataframe > thead > tr,\n.dataframe > tbody > tr {\n  text-align: right;\n  white-space: pre-wrap;\n}\n</style>\n<small>shape: (7, 3)</small><table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\"><thead><tr><th>label</th><th>type</th><th>nodes</th></tr><tr><td>str</td><td>str</td><td>list[str]</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&quot;M1&quot;</td><td>&quot;prime&quot;</td><td>[&quot;A&quot;, &quot;B&quot;, … &quot;E&quot;]</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;A&quot;</td><td>&quot;leaf&quot;</td><td>[&quot;A&quot;]</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;M2&quot;</td><td>&quot;parallel&quot;</td><td>[&quot;B&quot;, &quot;C&quot;]</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;B&quot;</td><td>&quot;leaf&quot;</td><td>[&quot;B&quot;]</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;C&quot;</td><td>&quot;leaf&quot;</td><td>[&quot;C&quot;]</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;D&quot;</td><td>&quot;leaf&quot;</td><td>[&quot;D&quot;]</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;E&quot;</td><td>&quot;leaf&quot;</td><td>[&quot;E&quot;]</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Interchangeable workers and tasks\n\n`teams()` links the 8 workers of a made-up software team to the 8 tasks of a release that\neach can do. A module of workers is a group that can all do the same tasks, and a module\nof tasks a group that the same workers can do:\n\n::: {#a362980a .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport polars as pl\n\nteam = ec.teams()\ntree = team.modular_decomposition()\n\n(\n    tree.get_node_df()\n    .filter(pl.col(\"type\") != \"leaf\")\n    .select(\"label\", \"type\", \"nodes\")\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```{=html}\n<div><style>\n.dataframe > thead > tr,\n.dataframe > tbody > tr {\n  text-align: right;\n  white-space: pre-wrap;\n}\n</style>\n<small>shape: (4, 3)</small><table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\"><thead><tr><th>label</th><th>type</th><th>nodes</th></tr><tr><td>str</td><td>str</td><td>list[str]</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&quot;M1&quot;</td><td>&quot;prime&quot;</td><td>[&quot;Priya&quot;, &quot;Tom&quot;, … &quot;Security&quot;]</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;M2&quot;</td><td>&quot;parallel&quot;</td><td>[&quot;Priya&quot;, &quot;Tom&quot;]</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;M3&quot;</td><td>&quot;parallel&quot;</td><td>[&quot;Sara&quot;, &quot;Hana&quot;]</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;M4&quot;</td><td>&quot;parallel&quot;</td><td>[&quot;Database&quot;, &quot;DevOps&quot;, &quot;Security&quot;]</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nPriya and Tom are interchangeable, and so are Sara and Hana. Database, DevOps and\nSecurity can only be done by Omar and Kofi:\n\n::: {#223139e6 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nmodules = {\n    member: row[\"label\"]\n    for row in tree.get_node_df().iter_rows(named=True)\n    if row[\"type\"] == \"parallel\"\n    for member in row[\"nodes\"]\n}\nteam.set_node_attrs(\"module\", modules)\nteam.layout_bipartite(align=\"horizontal\", aspect_ratio=0.4).plot(\n    layout=\"pos\", color_by=\"module\", label_by=\"label\", height=300,\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-a2be67a8:#d1d5db;--ect-0fa89f73:#929599;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: